
=== Sequence trimming

The purpose of this case study is to demonstrate the step-by-step development of
a Morloc CLI tool.

In this case study, we will be implementing a short-read quality trimmer. We
start be defining a single function that takes a minimum quality score and a
single quality vector as input. It reads through the scores and returns a range
specifying the bounds of the trimmed sequence.

==== Python base case trimmer

[source, python]
----
def trim_leading(min_qual, qual):
    for (i, q) in enumerate(qual):
        if q <= min_qual:
            return (i, len(qual))
    return (len(qual), len(qual))
----

This can be imported into morloc as so:

[source, morloc]
----
-- main.loc
module mtrim (leading)
import root-py
source Py from "trim.py" ("trim_leading" as leading)
leading :: U8 -> [U8] -> (U32, U32)
----

Now compile, print usage, and run:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc make -o trimmer main.loc                    # 0.084s
$ ./trimmer -h                                       # 0.004s
Usage: mtrim <nexus_options> @ <subcommand_options>

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')

Positional arguments:
  1:  type: U8
  2:  type: [U8]

Return: (U32, U32)

$ ./trimmer 30 example-scores.dat                    # 0.085ms
[9,12]
----

==== {cpp} base case trimmer

Rewrite the same function in C++

[source, cpp]
----
// trim.hpp
#pragma once
#include <cstdint>
#include <tuple>
#include <vector>

inline std::tuple<uint32_t, uint32_t>
trim_leading(uint8_t q, const std::vector<uint8_t>& qual) {
    uint32_t n = static_cast<uint32_t>(qual.size());
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        if (qual[i] >= q) return {i, n};
    return {n, n};
}
----

Change the source statements

[source, morloc]
----
-- main.loc
module mtrim (leading)
import root-cpp
source Cpp from "trim.hpp" ("trim_leading" as leading)
leading :: U8 -> [U8] -> (U32, U32)
----

Now compile again, print usage, and run:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc make -o trimmer main.loc                    # 0.684s
$ ./trimmer -h                                       # 0.004s
Usage: mtrim <nexus_options> @ <subcommand_options>

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')

Positional arguments:
  1:  type: U8
  2:  type: [U8]

Return: (U32, U32)

$ ./trimmer 30 example-scores.dat                    # 0.018ms
[2,9]
----

==== Docstrings

We document the types for a cleaner UI

[source, morloc]
----
module trimmers (leading)

import root-cpp

--' DNA sequence
type DNA = [U8]

--' A PHRED quality score
type Score = U8

--' Quality scores
type Scores = [Score]

--' Annotated read with quality scores
type Read a = (a, DNA, Scores)

--' Read interval bounds
type Bounds = (U32, U32)

source Cpp from "trim.hpp" ("trim_leading" as leading)

leading :: Score -> Scores -> Bounds
----

The docstrings propagate to the CLI usage statement

[source, console]
----
$ ./trimmers -h

Usage: trimmers <nexus_options> @ <subcommand_options>

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')

Positional arguments:
  1:  A PHRED quality score
      type: U8
  2:  Quality scores
      type: [Score]

Return: Bounds
  Read interval bounds
----

==== Map the trimmer over many reads

Now we can import the trimmer module and wrap it

[source, morloc]
----
module mtrim (trim)

import trimmers

--' language-agnostic function that applies trimmer to read
trimRead :: (Scores -> Bounds) -> Read a -> Read a
trimRead f r =
  let b = f (.2 r) -- find trimmed bounds
  in (.0 r, .1.[.0 b : .1 b] r, .2.[.0 b : .1 b] r)

--' Trim 0 or more reads
trim ::
  --' Minimum leading quality score
  U8 ->
  --' Untrimmed reads
  [Read Str] ->
  --' Trimmed reads
  [Read Str]
trim q = map (trimRead (leading q))
----


// ## FastQ and fast compression
//
// Let's write a quick FastQ parser
//
// ---haskell
// module (readFastQ)
//
// import root-cpp
//
// source Cpp from "fastq.hpp" ("readFastQ")
//
// --' Read a FastQ file
// readFastQ ::
//   --' FastQ filename
//   --' @check.path r
//   --' @metavar File
//   Str ->
//   --' list of fastq reads
//   <IO> [(Str, [U8], [U8])]
// ---
//
// Now we can read in FastQ files and write them to morloc binary
//
// ---console
// $ time zcat test.fastq.gz | ./fastq -f packet -z 3 @ - > test.dat #  23s
// $ time zcat test.fastq.gz > /dev/null                             #  10s
// $ time ./mtrim -f packet -z 3 trim 30 test.dat > trimmed.dat      #  26s
// $ time zcat test.fastq.gz | gzip > test.gz                        # 216s
// $ morloc-nexus view -f packet -z 1 test.dat > z.dat               #   4s
// $ morloc-nexus view -f packet -z 3 test.dat > z.dat               #  11s
// ---
//
// <div hidden>
// You may have noticed that I've been reading in FastQ scores as .dat files.
// </div>
//
// <div hidden>
// Now if we want compatibility with legacy formats, we could compose this with
// fastq readers and writers. But we DON'T want that, because fastq is a bad
// format. Instead we'll use our readers and writers only for converting fastq to
// and from morloc binary. We can directly export these two functions and
// recompile.
// </div>
//
// <div hidden>
// The morloc runtime that executes our programs offers many options for input. One
// is multi-threaded compression and decompression with the zstd library. We can
// parse the fastq format and write it to a compressed morloc data binary that can
// be directly read by other morloc tools. The data is stored in a form that can be
// directly copied into memory with minimal changes.
// </div>
//
// <div hidden>
// Why do this rather than using normal fastq with gzip compression? Because it is
// an order of magnitude faster (show benchmarks). Gzip is an ancient library. Why
// do we use it? We use it because every other tool uses it. We use it because it
// is a locked convention, and so we might easily spend an hour compressing a 60gb
// fastq file when we could do it in a minute just because gzip fits into our
// paradigm. In the morloc ecosystem, concerns such as compression are factored out
// and implemented once, so we can globally swap compression algorithms.
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.5 parallelism
//
// ---haskell
// module parallel (ppmap)
//
// source Cpp from "parallel.hpp" ("ppmap")
// ppmap :: (a -> b) -> [a] -> [b]
// ---
//
// <div hidden>
// So currently we have a trimmer that loops over on a single-thread over a list of
// reads. This is the MVP, but we can do this in parallel. Now there are a couple
// ways we could parallelize this. We could simply replace `map` with `pmap`, this
// would send each read off for processing on its own thread. Alternatively, we
// could chunk the reads into n pieces first (one for each thread) and process each
// chunk on a thread. Or we could iterate through the reads generating chunks of
// fixed size as we go. Let's do all three and compare their performance to the
// single-threaded solution.
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.6 logging/benchmarking, caching, cached debug traces
//
// <div hidden>
// Now we are running the trimmer on four implmentations simultaneously. Let's add
// in some logging.
// </div>
//
// <div hidden>
// So let's add a sharding function and pmap. These are both general purpose
// algorithms that can be reused on any problem.
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.7 streaming and file indexing
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.8 remote dispatch
//
// <div hidden>
// Remove dispatch is added in using the same approach. Let's say we want
// to process several files and dispatch each to a worker node. We can wrap
// our previous parallel tools in a loop that dispatches each, in parallel,
// to a worker node, then writes the results, and returns a bool on
// success. We can also add in logging and benchmarks as well.
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.9 Parameterization
//
//
//
// ---
//
// ## 3.10 composable CLIs, modules, and daemons
//
// <div hidden>
// So far, we've seen how to build CLI tools and workflows. But a morloc module can
// do more. First, it can be directly imported into other modules. In this way, we
// can build polyglot reuseable libraries. Also, we get composable CLI tools for
// free. You can build your own tool with exactly the functions you want and no
// undesired coupled dependencies. There is some startup cost to a morloc
// executable. This is small for compiled programs like our C++ trimmer, but for R
// or Python programs, that need to load modules, this can be a few seconds. So
// morloc supports a daemon mode for an executable. This allows microsecond latency
// communication with a modules exported functions through UNIX domain sockets, TCP
// or HTTP. In this way, Morloc can be used as a high-speed algorithmic backend for
// a dashboard or other app.
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 4.1 comparison of paradigms
//
// <div hidden>
// In the proposed paradigm, nodes in a workflow are idiomatic functions written in
// any supported language and edges are native data and their unambiguous serialized
// forms.
//
// Morloc supports this paradigm by providing a polyglot compiler
// </div>
//
// ---
//
// ## 4.2 universal library
//
//
