// ## Questions:
//
//  * So you want everyone to use this system? What about adoption?
//
//     But I don't want everyone. I don't need casual users, not yet, they would
//     frankly be a drag. What I need now are a few active people who can dig deep
//     into problems and work with me to explore the design space.
//
//  * Do languages matter?
//  * Why make a new binary format and not use X
//  * Why not just use Python?
//  * What about AI?
//  * Is it really polyglot?
//
//     There are three levels of polyglot:
//
//         - There are two dimensions here. Types and
//
//         - A system that passes strings/files between scripts in different
//           languages. Data flows between languages but each language must
//           independently process it. examples: Nextflow, Snakemake, polyglot
//           notebooks.
//
//         - A system that typechecks and compiles code in many languages into a
//           single binary - this is the truest polyglot system. From the user
//           perspective, Morloc achieves this, but under the hood, it still pays a
//           data marshalling price. This could be removed in the future if we
//           found a way to compile the code in all the supported languages into a
//           single binary (say WASM). If we did this, the frontend morloc user
//           would see no real difference in usage but a strong decrease in latency
//           for cross-language calls.
//
//  * Is this just system N?
//  *
//


